  "grammar_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Talking About the Past ('dzakagadzikwa')",
      "content": "The verb 'dzakagadzikwa' expresses a completed past action. Shona has two main ways to talk about the past: 1) For actions that happened earlier today or very recently, combine the short subject prefix ('nda-' = I, 'wa-' = you, 'a-' = he/she, 'ta-' = we, 'va-' = they) directly with the verb stem (e.g., 'ndamuka' = 'I woke up today', 'wabudirira' = 'you have succeeded'). 2) For events that happened yesterday or further in the past, insert '-ka-' after the subject prefix (e.g., 'takatenga' = 'we bought', 'vakaenda' = 'they went', 'akadanidzira' = 'he shouted').",
      "rule": "Recent Today Past: [nda- / wa- / ta- / va-] + verb | Earlier Past: [ndaka- / waka- / taka- / vaka-] + verb"
    },
    {
      "title": "Talking About the Future with '-cha-' ('uchangobva')",
      "content": "Look at 'uchangobva' in this sentence. Whenever you want to say what 'will' happen in the future, simply place the future marker '-cha-' between the subject prefix and the verb stem. For example, 'ndichabika' means 'I will cook', 'tichasvika' means 'we will arrive', and 'mvura ichanaya' means 'the rain will fall'. Pair it with 'mangwana' ('tomorrow') to talk about tomorrow's plans!",
      "rule": "Subject Prefix + -cha- + Verb Stem = Future ('will') -> Ndichaenda (I will go), Vachadzoka (They will return)"
    },
    {
      "title": "Colours & Describing Objects in Shona ('rwuneruvara')",
      "content": "This passage uses colour vocabulary such as 'rwuneruvara'. Shona has three traditional core colour adjectives that change their prefix slightly to match the noun they describe: '-chena' (white/clean, e.g., 'ruvara ruchena' or 'shangu chena'), '-tema / nhema' (black/dark, e.g., 'mota nhema' or 'ruvara rutema'), and '-tsvuku' (red/bright brown, e.g., 'mota tsvuku' or 'ruvara rutsvuku'). For other colours like green ('girinhi'), blue ('bhuruu'), or orange ('orenji'), speakers typically say 'ruvara rwe-' ('the colour of...') followed by the colour name, as in 'ruvara rwegirinhi' (green).",
      "rule": "Core Colours: -chena (white), -tema / nhema (black), -tsvuku (red) | Phrase pattern: ruvara rwe-[colour] (e.g., ruvara rwegirinhi)"
    }
  ],
  "cultural_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Cassava ('Mufarinya') & Root Crops in Zimbabwean Farming",
      "content": "Freshly harvested cassava ('mufarinya')—alongside sweet potatoes ('mbambaira') and taro ('madhumbe')—is a hearty root crop grown by smallholder farmers, especially in eastern and northern Zimbabwe, often boiled and enjoyed with morning tea.",
      "rule": null
    }
  ],
